{
 "id": "2026-10-09-ethiopia-eritrea",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Africa's second most populous country says it's being invaded. And the Red Sea sits right in the middle."
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Ethiopia told the UN Security Council it faces an all-out invasion by Eritrea. Its foreign minister says Eritrean troops pushed up to 60 kilometers into the north. Witnesses told Reuters they saw Eritrean soldiers in Adigrat, 25 kilometers from the border. Eritrea calls it a pretext for war."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "It lands on top of a civil war. Since September 23, the army has fought an alliance of seven armed groups, including the TPLF. This week it captured Tigray's capital, Mekelle. Addis Ababa says Eritrea, Egypt and Sudan back the rebels. All three deny it."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Underneath it all: the sea. Ethiopia lost its coast when Eritrea became independent in 1993. Now Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed insists his country of 130 million must reach the Red Sea."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Follow for the next update."
  }
 ],
